WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.095 --> 00:00:06.242
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.242 --> 00:00:11.372
We are now ready to learn how to search
for longest paths, not only

00:00:11.372 --> 00:00:16.560
in simple Manhattan line grids but in
arbitrary alignment graphs.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.510
Here's an example of a graph.

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:23.070
And, in this case, there is only one way

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:26.580
to move to these three nodes from the
source.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:30.350
And there is also one way to move to this
node from the source.

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:34.490
But when we move to this node,
shown by the question mark,

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:38.640
there are actually four different ways to
move to this node.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.250
Which one should we choose?

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:45.250
In this case, we actually
have four choices.

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:50.350
And as before, we choose the variant which
provides the longest path.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:55.040
In this case, this path is the optimal
one.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:56.480
And we know that the

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.428
optimal path from zero to the shown
node

00:01:00.428 --> 00:01:02.670
has length 10.

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:08.150
We continue doing this and in the end,
we will construct this graph.

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:14.210
with every note marked by the length
of the longest pass and we will

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:20.701
learn that the length of the longest pass
from the source to the send is 29.

00:01:20.701 --> 00:01:22.001
And as before,

00:01:22.001 --> 00:01:27.701
we of course preserved the backtracking
pointers and that's why we

00:01:27.701 --> 00:01:34.010
will know how to move back in this graph
and find the optimal path itself.

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:41.410
As a result, we now know how to
construct recurrency for the dynamic

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:47.310
programming in alignment graph.
If we define S(i,j) as the length of

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:53.480
a longest path from (0, 0) to (i, j).
Then in the alignment graph where

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:58.840
we have diagonal edges, corresponding to
the alignment gate, we have three choices.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:05.990
We can either compute S(i,j) as S(i-1) plus
the weight of the vertical edge

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:13.060
into node (i,j) or S(i-1,j) plus

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:21.170
plus weight of the horizontal edge into (i,j)
or S(i-1,j-1)

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:25.873
plus the weight of the diagonal edge into (i,j).

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:33.450
And as a result, our recurrency for the
longest common subsequence problem

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:38.950
becomes particularly simple, because the
weight of all edges

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:42.230
in the longest subsequence problem is
zero.

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:46.720
Except for the right edges, that
correspend to matching letters.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:51.472
And therefore the recurrency is simply
S(i-1, j) + 0

00:02:51.472 --> 00:02:54.620
or s(i,j-1) + 0.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:58.764
We can drop 0, of course, from this
equation, or

00:02:58.764 --> 00:03:04.540
s(i-1, j-1) + 1, in the case, i-th symbol
in the first sequence

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:09.000
equals to the j-th symbol in the second
sequence.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:14.700
and as before we, of course, preserve the

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:18.560
backtracking pointers for the longest
common subsequence.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:24.090
And in this case, the red edge has weight 1,
all the other edges have weight 0,

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:29.870
and here are the backtracking pointers for
our particular problem,

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:32.890
finding longest common subsequence for
these two strings.

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:38.540
And we can move from the final destination
backwards using

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:44.770
backtracking pointers, and slowly but
surely, we will arrive to

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:50.520
the original node, initial node, and all
passes

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:55.490
that are traced by the blue edges,
represent the solutions to

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:58.840
the longest common subsequence problem.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:03.720
To compute backtracking pointers, we have
three choices:

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:09.260
We can either record a backtracking
pointer as a vertical,

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:14.330
horizontal, or diagonal edge.
And as soon as the backtracking pointers

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:20.760
are computed we can write a simple program
that allows us to output the

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:26.230
longest common subsequence by, once again,
exploring three choices:

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:30.340
green choice, blue choice and red choice.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:34.740
We have now learned that to compute a
score at a

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:40.860
given node, we need to consider all edges
entering into this node.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:46.390
And the initial vertex of each such edge
is called a predecessor

00:04:46.390 --> 00:04:51.350
of a given node.
And therefore, the currency for

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:56.650
computing the longest paths in an
arbitrary graph will be the following one.

00:04:56.650 --> 00:05:01.620
Let's denote S(a) as the length of the
longest

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:07.070
path to node a, and to compute S(a), we
need to compute

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:12.700
the maximum among all predecessors of node A, and

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:18.210
we take maximum amounts of value scorer of
each predecessor plus

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:22.510
weight of the edge from this predecessor
to a given node.

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:27.630
So let's try to apply this approach to a
simple graph shown here.

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:32.814
We start from the initial node, and of
course, the length of the

00:05:32.814 --> 00:05:38.190
longest path to this node is 0, and we can
compute two other scores

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:42.414
for two other nodes, 4 and 6, and now,
let's try

00:05:42.414 --> 00:05:47.041
to compute the score for the node shown
by the question mark.

00:05:47.041 --> 00:05:50.910
There are two edges entering into this
node.

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:52.220
Score of one of them,

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:57.390
of one predecessor is known as zero, but
score of another

00:05:57.390 --> 00:06:03.430
predecessor is unknown, therefore before
we compute the score of the node

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:09.170
shown by the question mark, we need to
compute one more score, which is this one.

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:12.950
But to compute this score we need

00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:15.480
to compute score in another vertex,
shown here.

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:22.070
But to compute score of the vertex, we
need to compute score for this one.

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:24.380
And to compute score of for this one, we
need to

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:28.800
compute the score that we had been trying
to compute some

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:32.640
time ago.
So we came to a vicious circle.

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.937
It looks like a catch-22.

00:06:34.937 --> 00:06:39.081
We need to compute something that has not
been computed yet, but to

00:06:39.081 --> 00:06:43.750
compute it, we need to compute something
else again and again and again.

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:48.500
Which tells us that the order in

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:54.220
which we explore nodes of the graph is
extremely important.

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:59.270
By the time the note is analyzed, the
score of all its predecessors should

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:04.750
already have been computed.
And if the graph has a directed cycle,

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:10.320
like the graph you just looked at, there
is simply no way to solve the problem.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:16.970
Because a traveler may travel along this
directed cycle as long as he wants to.

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:19.385
And therefore, they limit attention to

00:07:19.385 --> 00:07:24.490
so-called directed acyclic graphs, or
DAGs, which are

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:30.010
graphs without directed cycles.

00:07:30.010 --> 00:07:34.510
In this slide, Batman is looking at a
directed acyclic graph.

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:39.160
And is trying to solve a difficult
dressing problem in the morning.

00:07:40.630 --> 00:07:44.680
And topological ordering of a graph is
an ordering

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:49.760
of nodes of the DAG on a line such that

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:53.950
all edges in the graph go from left to
right.

00:07:53.950 --> 00:07:57.110
There are different ways for Batman
to get

00:07:57.110 --> 00:08:00.060
dressed in the morning, two of them are
shown here,

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:05.060
and each of them represents a topological
ordering because by the

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:10.350
time Batman needs to put something on,
all necessary

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:14.680
things already have been put on in this
particular case.

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:18.300
And, there is a simple theorem, that we
are not proving but you

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:23.700
can try to prove it to yourself, that
every DAG has the topological ordering.

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:27.600
And in fact, for our Manhattan grids,

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:30.450
there are many different ordering,
topological orderings.

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:33.350
And two of them are shown on this slide.

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:36.270
And after

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:39.860
we learned the topological ordering is
important,

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:43.740
we can design the pseudocode for the longest
path problem.

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:45.010
And it works like this.

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:48.540
We first topologically order the graph.

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:53.210
And then we update nodes in the order
given

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:57.710
by this topological ordering as described
on this slide.

00:08:57.710 --> 00:09:01.550
What is the runtime of this algorithm that
we will use heavily

00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:03.480
in the remainder of this lecture?

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:10.100
Well, when we run dynamic programming, we
look at every

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:16.750
edge in the graph just once to update the
node that this edge goes into.

00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:21.150
And therefore, at the runtime of the
dynamic programming algorithm for finding

00:09:21.150 --> 00:09:26.030
longest paths is proportional to the
number of edges in the graph.

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:26.580
And we are now

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:31.330
ready to look at different varieties of
sequence alignment problems

00:09:31.330 --> 00:09:33.410
in biology.

